meta {
  name: Find the 2019 annual paper
  type: http
  seq: 12
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/library?kind=paper&exam_type=annual&exam_year=2019
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.count: eq 1
  res.body.content_items[0].id: eq {{paperItemId}}
  res.body.content_items[0].paper.paper_set: eq A
}

docs {
  This is the search the exam metadata exists for: last year's annual paper for
  this subject, without knowing its title. exam_type, board and exam_year read
  through to the sidecar, so they filter items by the paper attached to them
  rather than needing a separate papers endpoint with its own scoping rules.

  Every row that comes back is one the caller may read. The narrowing is in the
  query, not applied to the page afterwards, so counts here are counts of
  readable things -- a filter that ran after the fact would page over rows the
  caller cannot see and return short pages with no explanation.

  exam_year must be a positive year. A zero or a word is a 400 rather than a
  filter that quietly matches nothing.
}
